// Windows line endings. Only the line feed starts a new line, so the carriage return of a CRLF pair is the last byte of
// the line it ends and the token after the pair stands in column 1 of the next line. A lone carriage return breaks
// nothing: the second line of the input carries one in the middle, and the columns after it keep counting from the
// start of that line.
//
// A backend which takes the carriage return for a line break, or which strips it before it counts, produces different
// columns here and nowhere else in the corpus. The spans are untouched by any of it, because a span is counted in
// bytes and a line ending is bytes like any other.
//
// The scanner is not what this case is about, so its rules are single characters and runs of digits. Its whitespace
// rule has to accept the carriage return, or the input would be a scanner error instead of the line endings it is
// about.

@scanner {
    WHITESPACE: /[ \t\r\n]+/ @skip;

    NUMBER:    /[0-9]+/;
    SEMICOLON: ";";
}

@parser {
    list
        : list item
        | item
        ;

    item
        : NUMBER ";"
        ;
}
